<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The Android face of the mobile shell. Everything shared with iOS lives in `src/Shenora.Mobile/`
    and is compiled in by the import below; this project holds the TFM, the platform floor, the
    package identity, and anything under `Platforms/` that only Android can do.
  -->
  <Import Project="..\Shenora.Mobile\Shenora.Mobile.props" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0-android</TargetFrameworks>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>21.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <!--
      No generated `Resource` designer class. This library ships no Android resources, but the SDK
      emits `public partial class Resource` regardless — accidental PUBLIC API that nobody designed,
      that appeared in the API baseline, and that has no iOS counterpart. Found by rendering the real
      iOS assembly against the Android baseline: the two differed by exactly `Shenora.Mobile.Resource`.
      Turning it off deletes a public type we never meant to ship AND makes the two mobile surfaces
      genuinely identical, which is what lets the Android baseline gate iOS from a Windows host.
      Re-enable the moment this project gains a Resources/ folder.
    -->
    <AndroidGenerateResourceDesigner>false</AndroidGenerateResourceDesigner>
    <!-- Inline on purpose — the baseline coverage gate text-matches it here. See Shenora.Mobile.props. -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Shenora.Android</PackageId>
    <Description>Shenora's Android shell: the MAUI HybridWebView IPC transport (the same envelope the desktop shell speaks, over SendRawMessage/RawMessageReceived), the resource interceptor that answers a page's byte ranges, a UI-thread dispatcher, safe-area insets, and the Android implementations of the Shenora shell contracts — native file dialogs, a MediaCodec-backed audio and video conversion pipeline with device capability probing, an ExoPlayer/MediaPlayer media player, and a MediaSession playback session for the lock screen and headphone controls. The platform owns the loop, so the app drives ShenoraApplication.Start/Stop from its own lifecycle rather than calling Run. Shares its source with Shenora.iOS.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
